            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"God's unmerited favor and free gift of salvation, with some traditions also emphasizing it as an empowering presence that enables holiness and obedience.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Favor that both initiates salvation and actively teaches the recipient to live righteously, contrasting with 'counterfeit grace' that acts merely as a pardon without producing behavioral change.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Unmerited divine favor granting salvation and forgiveness.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Favor that moves first and then teaches the rejection of ungodliness.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book excludes any concept of grace that functions solely as a pardon without also actively teaching and producing righteous living, labeling such a truncated view 'counterfeit grace.'\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book does not contradict the core idea of favor, but it rejects definitions that separate grace's saving pardon from its ongoing, sanctifying instruction.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Both the book and consensus agree that grace is divine favor. The book refines this by elevating the sanctifying function of grace (explicit in Titus 2 and noted as a secondary or alternative consensus view) to a definitional requirement, thereby narrowing true grace to exclude views that see it only as unmerited pardon.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The book's definition might be criticized for conflating justification (unmerited pardon) with sanctification (empowerment for living), which some Protestant traditions explicitly distinguish to protect the 'free gift' nature of grace.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
